## What I did

Finished the code landing for defect #2, `D-MOUNT-INCARNATION-CONSTANT-ZERO` — all five remaining items from the sess86 plan. **It builds clean** (`make clean && make modules`, zero errors, zero new warnings in `dlm/`). VERSION 0.11.420, `MXFS_PROTO_GEN` 2→3.

- `confirm_dead_mask` exports `out_epoch` — the baseline incarnation held frozen across the confirmation window, which is the one actually proven dead.
- `dlm/mount.c` TCP callback converted to the 4-arg signature; that path is node-scoped and marks nothing pending, so the identity is logged and otherwise unused.
- `dlm/v5_mount.c`: `mount_stale_epoch[]` added; `start`/`defer_slice_recovery` forward `victim_epoch`; the expire callback is split into a 2-arg lease form and a 4-arg disklock form over a shared body; `recovery_complete` captures the victim epoch once at entry, uses compare-and-clear at all three sites, and handles `MXFS_RECOVERY_SUPERSEDED` by returning 0 rather than taking the retry path (which would livelock against a live node).

**One deliberate divergence from the banked plan**, which I flagged before implementing: the plan said the lease path should resolve the victim epoch via `mxfs_disklock_node_epoch()`. That function returned "who is in this slot *now*", not "who died" — the same category error this whole change removes from `mark_recovery_pending`. Using it would name a live successor as victim *with `inc_valid` set*, so `recovery_begin`'s incarnation test would match and publish a guard against a healthy member with every appearance of having been verified. The lease path passes 0 instead (`P237-RECOV-INC-UNOBSERVED` — same behavior as before this campaign, labelled unproven rather than fabricated). I deleted `node_epoch()` and replaced it with a header comment saying why no such accessor may exist.

Also fixed `v5_dispatch_late_deaths`, which named the victim from a snapshot that goes stale if the slot was re-armed.

